{"task_id": "HumanEval/3", "solution": "```python\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"balance-checker\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Detects if a sequence of bank account operations causes the running balance to fall below zero.\"\nauthors = [{ name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" }]\ndependencies = [\n    \"fastapi>=0.110.0\",\n    \"uvicorn[standard]>=0.29.0\",\n    \"pydantic>=2.0.0\",\n]\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\nlicense = { text = \"MIT\" }\n\n[project.scripts]\nbalance-check = \"balance_checker.cli:main\"\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n```\n\n```markdown\n# README.md\n\n## Balance Checker\n\nA Python library, CLI tool, and HTTP API to detect if a sequence of bank account operations causes the running balance to fall below zero at any point.\n\n### Installation\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/your-repo/balance-checker.git\ncd balance-checker\npip install .\n```\n\n### Library Usage\n\n```python\nfrom balance_checker import below_zero, InvalidOperationError\n\n# Example 1: Balance never goes below zero\noperations1 = [100, -20, 50, -30]\nresult1 = below_zero(operations1)\nprint(f\"Operations {operations1} result: {result1}\") # Expected: False\n\n# Example 2: Balance goes below zero\noperations2 = [100, -20, -90, 50] # Balance: 100 -> 80 -> -10 (below zero)\nresult2 = below_zero(operations2)\nprint(f\"Operations {operations2} result: {result2}\") # Expected: True\n\n# Example 3: Invalid input\ntry:\n    below_zero([10, \"invalid\", -5])\nexcept InvalidOperationError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\") # Expected: Error: element at index 1 is not an integer\n\ntry:\n    below_zero([])\nexcept InvalidOperationError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\") # Expected: Error: operations list must not be empty\n```\n\n### CLI Usage\n\n```bash\n# Check operations (space-separated)\nbalance-check 100 -20 50 -30\n# Output: False\n\nbalance-check 100 -20 -90 50\n# Output: True\n\n# Check operations (comma-separated or single string argument)\nbalance-check \"100,-20,-90,50\"\n# Output: True\n\nbalance-check \"[10,20,-50,100]\"\n# Output: True\n\n# Invalid input\nbalance-check 10 invalid -5\n# Output to stderr: Invalid input: element at index 1 is not an integer\n# Exit code: 2\n```\n\n### HTTP API Usage\n\nRun the API:\n```bash\nuvicorn src.balance_checker.api:create_app --factory --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000\n```\n\nThen, you can use `curl` or any HTTP client:\n\n```bash\n# Example 1: Balance never goes below zero\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/below_zero -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '[100, -20, 50, -30]'\n# Response: {\"below_zero\": false}\n\n# Example 2: Balance goes below zero\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/below_zero -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '[100, -20, -90, 50]'\n# Response: {\"below_zero\": true}\n\n# Example 3: Invalid input\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/below_zero -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '[10, \"invalid\", -5]'\n# Response (400 Bad Request): {\"detail\":\"element at index 1 is not an integer\"}\n```\n```\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/__init__.py\nfrom typing import List\n\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidOperationError\nfrom .services import BalanceService\nfrom .validators import OperationValidator\n\n_service_instance = BalanceService()\n_validator_instance = OperationValidator()\n\n\ndef below_zero(operations: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Detects if a sequence of bank account operations causes the balance to fall below zero.\n    Validates input and returns True on first negative balance, otherwise False.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        validated_operations = _validator_instance.validate(operations)\n        return _service_instance.below_zero(validated_operations)\n    except InvalidOperationError:\n        raise\n    except Exception as e:\n        raise RuntimeError(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\") from e\n\n__all__ = [\"below_zero\", \"InvalidOperationError\"]\n```\n\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/api.py\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException\nfrom typing import List\nfrom .services import BalanceService\nfrom .validators import OperationValidator\nfrom .models import BelowZeroResult\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidOperationError\nimport logging\n\n# Configure logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format='%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s')\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\ndef create_app() -> FastAPI:\n    \"\"\"\n    Creates and configures the FastAPI application.\n    The BalanceService and OperationValidator are instantiated internally for simplicity,\n    but could be injected using a dependency provider pattern for more complex scenarios.\n    \"\"\"\n    app = FastAPI(\n        title=\"Balance Checker API\",\n        description=\"API to check if a sequence of bank account operations results in a below zero balance.\",\n        version=\"0.1.0\"\n    )\n\n    # Instantiate dependencies\n    # As per NFR2 (Testable: dependency injection), we would inject these in a real app.\n    # For a factory function returning app, we can instantiate them here.\n    balance_service = BalanceService()\n    operation_validator = OperationValidator()\n\n    @app.post(\"/below_zero\", response_model=BelowZeroResult, status_code=200)\n    async def below_zero_endpoint(operations: List[int]):\n        \"\"\"\n        Receives a list of integer operations and returns whether the balance\n        falls below zero at any point.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            # The input operations: List[int] is automatically validated by Pydantic\n            # However, our OperationValidator has additional logic (e.g., allow_empty=False)\n            # and provides more specific error messages.\n            validated_ops = operation_validator.validate(operations)\n            result_bool = balance_service.below_zero(validated_ops)\n            return BelowZeroResult(below_zero=result_bool)\n        except InvalidOperationError as e:\n            logger.warning(f\"Bad Request: {e.message}\")\n            raise HTTPException(status_code=400, detail=e.message)\n        except Exception as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Internal Server Error: {e}\", exc_info=True)\n            raise HTTPException(status_code=500, detail=\"Internal server error\")\n\n    return app\n\n# If running directly with `uvicorn api:app`, use this:\n# app = create_app()\n# However, the design specifies `create_app` as a factory,\n# so `uvicorn src.balance_checker.api:create_app --factory` is the recommended way.\n```\n\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/cli.py\nimport sys\nimport argparse\nimport logging\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom .controllers import CLIController\nfrom .services import BalanceService\nfrom .validators import OperationValidator\nfrom .utils import parse_operations_arg\nfrom .repositories import FileOperationRepository\n\n# Configure basic logging for CLI\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.WARNING, format='%(levelname)s: %(message)s')\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Main entry point for the CLI tool.\n    Parses command-line arguments and invokes the CLIController.\n    \"\"\"\n    argv = argv if argv is not None else sys.argv[1:]\n\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Check if a sequence of bank account operations causes the balance to fall below zero.\",\n        epilog=\"Examples:\\n  balance-check 10 20 -50 30\\n  balance-check \\\"10,20,-50,30\\\"\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"operations\",\n        nargs=\"+\",\n        help=\"A list of integer operations (deposits as positive, withdrawals as negative). \"\n             \"Can be space-separated (e.g., '10 20 -50') or a single string \"\n             \"with comma-separated values (e.g., '\\\"10,20,-50\\\"').\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--log-file\",\n        type=str,\n        help=f\"Path to a file for logging operations. Defaults to '{FileOperationRepository.DEFAULT_LOG_PATH}'.\",\n        default=None\n    )\n\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n\n    raw_input_str = \" \".join(args.operations)\n    try:\n        ops = parse_operations_arg(raw_input_str)\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.error(f\"Invalid input: {e}\")\n        return 2 # Exit code for invalid input\n\n    # Instantiate repository if log_file is provided\n    repository = None\n    if args.log_file:\n        try:\n            repository = FileOperationRepository(log_path=args.log_file)\n            logger.info(f\"Operations will be logged to: {args.log_file}\")\n        except Exception as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Failed to initialize log file repository: {e}\")\n            return 1 # Exit code for other errors\n\n    # Dependency injection: instantiate BalanceService with the optional repository\n    balance_service = BalanceService(repository=repository)\n    operation_validator = OperationValidator()\n    \n    controller = CLIController(service=balance_service, validator=operation_validator)\n    return controller.run(ops)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/controllers.py\nimport sys\nimport logging\nfrom typing import List\n\nfrom .services import BalanceService\nfrom .validators import OperationValidator\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidOperationError\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass CLIController:\n    \"\"\"\n    Orchestrates the CLI flow: validation, service call, and output formatting.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, service: BalanceService, validator: OperationValidator):\n        self.service = service\n        self.validator = validator\n\n    def run(self, operations: List[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Executes the main logic for the CLI.\n        Validates operations, calls the BalanceService, prints the result, and returns an exit code.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            # CLI main function already parses and partially validates;\n            # this validator is for the core business logic boundary.\n            validated_ops = self.validator.validate(operations)\n            result = self.service.below_zero(validated_ops)\n            print(result) # Print True/False to stdout\n            return 0 # Success\n        except InvalidOperationError as e:\n            print(f\"Error: {e.message}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 2 # Invalid input\n        except Exception as e:\n            logger.error(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", exc_info=True)\n            print(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 1 # General error\n```\n\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/services.py\nimport logging\nfrom typing import List, Iterator, Optional\n\nfrom .repositories import OperationRepository\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidOperationError\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass BalanceService:\n    \"\"\"\n    Core business logic to detect if running balance falls below zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, repository: Optional[OperationRepository] = None):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initializes the BalanceService with an optional OperationRepository.\n        Args:\n            repository: An optional repository for logging operations.\n        \"\"\"\n        self._repository = repository\n\n    def cumulative_balances(self, operations: List[int]) -> Iterator[int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Generator that yields the running cumulative balance after each operation.\n        Args:\n            operations: A list of integer operations.\n        Yields:\n            The current cumulative balance.\n        \"\"\"\n        running_balance = 0\n        for op in operations:\n            running_balance += op\n            yield running_balance\n\n    def below_zero(self, operations: List[int]) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if the cumulative balance falls below zero at any point.\n        Logs the operations sequence to the repository if one is provided.\n        Args:\n            operations: A list of integer operations.\n        Returns:\n            True if the balance falls below zero, False otherwise.\n        Raises:\n            InvalidOperationError: If operations is None.\n        \"\"\"\n        if operations is None:\n            raise InvalidOperationError(\"operations cannot be None\")\n\n        result = False\n        for balance in self.cumulative_balances(operations):\n            if balance < 0:\n                result = True\n                break\n        \n        # Log operations regardless of outcome, as it's an audit log\n        if self._repository:\n            try:\n                # The spec suggests logging on first negative balance OR after loop.\n                # A single log at the end after determining the final boolean is simpler\n                # and still captures the audit requirement. Adding result to log entry.\n                self._repository.save(operations, result) \n            except Exception as e:\n                logger.warning(f\"Failed to log operations to repository: {e}\")\n                # Do not re-raise, as logging is non-critical for core business logic\n\n        return result\n```\n\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/models.py\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import List\n\n@dataclass\nclass OperationSequence:\n    \"\"\"\n    Domain model for a sequence of bank account operations.\n    \"\"\"\n    operations: List[int]\n\n@dataclass\nclass BelowZeroResult:\n    \"\"\"\n    DTO for the result indicating if the balance fell below zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    below_zero: bool\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"Converts the result to a JSON-serializable dictionary.\"\"\"\n        return {\"below_zero\": self.below_zero}\n```\n\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/validators.py\nfrom typing import Any, List\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidOperationError\n\nclass OperationValidator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates input operation sequences to ensure they are valid for processing.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, allow_empty: bool = False):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initializes the validator.\n        Args:\n            allow_empty: If True, an empty list of operations is considered valid.\n                         Defaults to False (empty list is invalid).\n        \"\"\"\n        self.allow_empty = allow_empty\n\n    def validate(self, operations: Any) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates the given input as a list of integers.\n        Args:\n            operations: The input to validate, expected to be a list-like structure.\n        Returns:\n            A validated list of integers.\n        Raises:\n            InvalidOperationError: If the input is not a list, contains non-integers,\n                                   or is empty when allow_empty is False.\n        \"\"\"\n        if operations is None:\n            raise InvalidOperationError(\"operations must be provided (cannot be None)\")\n        \n        # Ensure it's a list or tuple for iteration, but ideally list.\n        # FastAPI's List[int] Pydantic validation handles much of this, but CLI/library needs it.\n        if not isinstance(operations, (list, tuple)):\n            raise InvalidOperationError(\"operations must be a list of integers\")\n        \n        if not operations and not self.allow_empty:\n            raise InvalidOperationError(\"operations list must not be empty\")\n        \n        # Ensure all elements are integers\n        result = []\n        for i, v in enumerate(operations):\n            if not isinstance(v, int):\n                raise InvalidOperationError(f\"element at index {i} is not an integer (value: {v})\")\n            result.append(v)\n        \n        return result\n```\n\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/repositories.py\nimport json\nimport logging\nfrom typing import List\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass OperationRepository:\n    \"\"\"\n    Abstract base class (interface) for persisting operation sequences.\n    \"\"\"\n    def save(self, operations: List[int], result: bool) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Saves a sequence of operations with its associated below_zero result.\n        Args:\n            operations: The list of operations performed.\n            result: The boolean result from the below_zero check.\n        \"\"\"\n        raise NotImplementedError\n\nclass FileOperationRepository(OperationRepository):\n    \"\"\"\n    Concrete implementation of OperationRepository that saves operations\n    to a newline-delimited JSON log file.\n    \"\"\"\n    DEFAULT_LOG_PATH = \"operation_logs.log\"\n\n    def __init__(self, log_path: str = DEFAULT_LOG_PATH):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initializes the file repository.\n        Args:\n            log_path: The path to the log file.\n        \"\"\"\n        self._log_path = log_path\n        # Ensure the log file exists or can be created\n        try:\n            with open(self._log_path, \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                f.write(\"\") # Just touch the file\n        except IOError as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Could not access or create log file at {self._log_path}: {e}\")\n            raise # Re-raise if file cannot be initialized\n\n    def save(self, operations: List[int], result: bool) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Appends a JSON record of the operations and result to the log file.\n        Each record includes a timestamp.\n        Args:\n            operations: The list of operations performed.\n            result: The boolean result from the below_zero check.\n        \"\"\"\n        record = {\n            \"timestamp\": datetime.utcnow().isoformat() + \"Z\",\n            \"operations\": operations,\n            \"below_zero_result\": result\n        }\n        try:\n            with open(self._log_path, \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                f.write(json.dumps(record) + \"\\n\")\n            logger.debug(f\"Logged operations to {self._log_path}\")\n        except IOError as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Failed to write to operation log file '{self._log_path}': {e}\")\n            # Do not re-raise, as per spec: \"catch IO errors and log warnings; do not raise to service\"\n        except Exception as e:\n            logger.error(f\"An unexpected error occurred while saving operations: {e}\")\n```\n\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/exceptions.py\nclass InvalidOperationError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"\n    Exception raised for invalid bank account operations or input sequences.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initializes the InvalidOperationError.\n        Args:\n            message: A descriptive error message.\n        \"\"\"\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\n```\n\n```python\n# src/balance_checker/utils.py\nimport json\nfrom typing import List\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidOperationError\n\ndef parse_operations_arg(arg: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string argument representing bank operations into a list of integers.\n    Supports space-separated, comma-separated, or JSON array string formats.\n    Args:\n        arg: The string argument from the CLI.\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers.\n    Raises:\n        InvalidOperationError: If the string cannot be parsed into a list of integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    parts: List[str] = []\n\n    # Attempt to parse as JSON array first, e.g., \"[1,2,-4]\"\n    if arg.startswith('[') and arg.endswith(']'):\n        try:\n            json_list = json.loads(arg)\n            if not isinstance(json_list, list):\n                raise InvalidOperationError(\"JSON input must be an array.\")\n            # Convert numbers to strings for consistent int conversion later\n            parts = [str(x) for x in json_list]\n        except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError) as e:\n            # If it's not a valid JSON array, try other formats\n            pass\n    \n    if not parts: # If not parsed as JSON, or JSON parsing failed, try other delimiters\n        if ',' in arg:\n            parts = [p.strip() for p in arg.split(',')]\n        else:\n            parts = [p.strip() for p in arg.split()]\n    \n    if not parts:\n        raise InvalidOperationError(\"No operations provided or could not parse input.\")\n\n    operations: List[int] = []\n    for i, p in enumerate(parts):\n        try:\n            operations.append(int(p))\n        except ValueError:\n            raise InvalidOperationError(f\"element at index {i} is not a valid integer: '{p}'\")\n    \n    return operations\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) of a list of numbers around their mean.\n    MAD is the average absolute difference between each data point and the dataset's mean.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n    current_mean = sum(numbers) / n\n\n    total_absolute_deviation = 0.0\n    for x in numbers:\n        total_absolute_deviation += abs(x - current_mean)\n\n    mad = total_absolute_deviation / n\n    return mad\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Insert a number 'delimeter' between every two consecutive elements of input list `numbers`.\n    For an empty or single-element list, no delimiter is inserted.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    result: List[int] = []\n    it = iter(numbers)\n    result.append(next(it))\n    for x in it:\n        result.append(delimeter)\n        result.append(x)\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Insert a number 'delimeter' between every two consecutive elements of input list `numbers'.\n    >>> intersperse([], 4)\n    []\n    >>> intersperse([1, 2, 3], 4)\n    [1, 4, 2, 4, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return list(numbers)\n\n    result = []\n    for i, val in enumerate(numbers):\n        result.append(val)\n        if i < n - 1:\n            result.append(delimeter)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\nimport math\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\" For a given list of input numbers, calculate Mean Absolute Deviation\n    around the mean of this dataset.\n    Mean Absolute Deviation is the average absolute difference between each\n    element and a centerpoint (mean in this case):\n    MAD = average | x - x_mean |\n    >>> mean_absolute_deviation([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = math.fsum(numbers)\n    mean = total_sum / len(numbers)\n    abs_diff_sum = math.fsum(abs(x - mean) for x in numbers)\n    return abs_diff_sum / len(numbers)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Computes the maximum nesting depth for multiple parenthesis groups.\n    Each group is separated by spaces, and the depth for each group is returned as a list of integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    groups = paren_string.split()\n    depths = []\n\n    for group in groups:\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth_for_group = 0\n        for char in group:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n                if current_depth > max_depth_for_group:\n                    max_depth_for_group = current_depth\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_depth -= 1\n        depths.append(max_depth_for_group)\n\n    return depths\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Input is space-separated groups of parentheses.\n    Calculates the deepest nesting level for each group.\n    \"\"\"\n    results: List[int] = []\n    # Using split() without arguments handles multiple spaces and trims empty groups automatically.\n    groups = paren_string.split()\n\n    for group in groups:\n        balance = 0\n        max_depth = 0\n        for char in group:\n            if char == '(':\n                balance += 1\n                max_depth = max(max_depth, balance)\n            elif char == ')':\n                balance -= 1\n            # Per critical requirements, no error handling for invalid characters.\n        results.append(max_depth)\n    return results\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/7", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef filter_by_substring(strings: List[str], substring: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\" Filter an input list of strings only for ones that contain given substring.\n    This function performs a case-sensitive match and preserves order.\n    >>> filter_by_substring([], 'a')\n    []\n    >>> filter_by_substring(['abc', 'bacd', 'cde', 'array'], 'a')\n    ['abc', 'bacd', 'array']\n    \"\"\"\n    if substring == \"\":\n        return strings[:]\n    return [s for s in strings if substring in s]\n```"}
